{"chapter": "formal-defos", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "06GB", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$\\mathcal{C}_\\Lambda$", "STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:fb6ad280346362a580dd3b1ba5464da5eea3146f40ecf0a8f3a682476d6fa006", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:9ba98184b0c4f8ab38dddd8fc206b0d08ceeb6c6a96b3ce916241f7d699f851a", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nBy the following lemma we can often reduce arguments involving surjective ring\nmaps in <MATH_0001> to the case of small extensions.\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:83935f4cc1ee811eb3b61444ac9d006bc5b434c5cda62b2b0b1572354c1b9ad2", "unit_id": "tag:06GB:prose-0003"}
{"chapter": "formal-defos", "node_kind": "lemma", "parent_tag": "06GB", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$f: B \\to A$", "MATH_0002": "$\\mathcal{C}_\\Lambda$", "MATH_0003": "$f$"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{lemma}\n\\label{lemma-factor-small-extension}\n", "suffix": "\\end{lemma}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:9b658df56608e99e9614d6ca34665c242f6153a2366a8a2b815900721e408567", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:3f3c6203c5898ffadca2852967ad32bd9ad825d03d321c03555d1e3adee4dc1b", "source_text": "Let <MATH_0001> be a surjective ring map in <MATH_0002>.\nThen <MATH_0003> can be factored as a composition of small extensions.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:5f2602edf122be9f8ad199f862e6b258c8e4ee7a1a3f1c6b0d7effaf2179e2fb", "unit_id": "tag:06GE:statement"}
{"chapter": "formal-defos", "node_kind": "proof", "parent_tag": "06GB", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$I$", "MATH_0002": "$f$", "MATH_0003": "$\\mathfrak{m}_B$", "MATH_0004": "$B$", "MATH_0005": "$\\mathfrak{m}_B^n = 0$", "MATH_0006": "$$\nB = B/I\\mathfrak{m}_B^{n-1} \\to B/I\\mathfrak{m}_B^{n-2} \\to\n\\ldots \\to B/I \\cong A\n$$", "MATH_0007": "$f$", "MATH_0008": "$f$", "MATH_0009": "$I$", "MATH_0010": "$\\mathfrak{m}_B$", "MATH_0011": "$I$", "MATH_0012": "$k$", "MATH_0013": "$x_1, \\ldots, x_n$", "MATH_0014": "$I$", "MATH_0015": "$k$", "MATH_0016": "$$\nB \\to B/(x_1) \\to \\ldots \\to  B/(x_1, \\ldots, x_n) \\cong  A\n$$", "MATH_0017": "$f$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{algebra-lemma-artinian-finite-length}", "SPACE_0001": "\\ "}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{proof}\n", "suffix": "\\end{proof}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:d833345068c3f956ebe09c9db697ff343e3c7c1c6c0645a42111b136d3bc8027", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:97ef719b29600b4c74be51421fe2e743bf5182afad70b9b7b656557662fbeea8", "source_text": "Let <MATH_0001> be the kernel of <MATH_0002>.  The maximal ideal <MATH_0003> is\nnilpotent since <MATH_0004> is Artinian, say <MATH_0005>. Hence we get a\nfactorization\n<MATH_0006>\nof <MATH_0007> into a composition of surjective maps whose kernels are annihilated by\nthe maximal ideal.  Thus it suffices to prove the lemma when <MATH_0008> itself is such\na map, i.e.<SPACE_0001>when <MATH_0009> is annihilated by <MATH_0010>. In this case\n<MATH_0011> is a <MATH_0012>-vector space, which has finite dimension, see\nAlgebra, Lemma <REF_0001>.\nTake a basis <MATH_0013> of <MATH_0014> as a <MATH_0015>-vector space to get a\nfactorization\n<MATH_0016>\nof <MATH_0017> into a composition of small extensions.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:ad9b3a927531b1599b533471cf4596c2ffc5bfca8b9ad7644a51ca21d5e83947", "unit_id": "tag:06GE:proof"}
{"chapter": "formal-defos", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "06GB", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$\\Lambda$", "MATH_0002": "$k$", "MATH_0003": "$\\Lambda$", "MATH_0004": "$k' \\subset k$", "MATH_0005": "$\\Lambda \\to k$", "MATH_0006": "$k' \\subset k$", "MATH_0007": "$k'$", "MATH_0008": "$k/k'$", "MATH_0009": "$\\Lambda \\to k'$", "MATH_0010": "$\\mathfrak m_\\Lambda$", "MATH_0011": "\\begin{equation}\n\\label{equation-k-prime}\n[k : k'] = [k : \\Lambda/\\mathfrak m_\\Lambda] < \\infty\n\\end{equation}", "MATH_0012": "$k = k'$", "MATH_0013": "$k' = \\Lambda/\\mathfrak m_\\Lambda$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{algebra-lemma-integral-under-field}", "STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:2849fc4184d46167b9ebf70c8d37068c606bf64b908798158ef63bf2db2b6a3c", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:9d64a9ebbc1b15e0f3f1d5cfbe1af5e1e0d06a8c0a49618e6a9870856b1ce38d", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nThe next lemma says that we can compute the length of a module over a local\n<MATH_0001>-algebra with residue field <MATH_0002> in terms of the length over\n<MATH_0003>. To explain the notation in the statement, let <MATH_0004>\nbe the image of our fixed finite ring map <MATH_0005>. Note\nthat <MATH_0006> is a finite extension of rings. Hence <MATH_0007> is a field\nand <MATH_0008> is a finite extension of fields, see\nAlgebra, Lemma <REF_0001>.\nMoreover, as <MATH_0009> is surjective we see that its kernel\nis a maximal ideal <MATH_0010>. Thus\n<MATH_0011>\nand in the classical case we have <MATH_0012>. The notation\n<MATH_0013> will be fixed throughout this chapter.\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:807a155f900e1835a3b1462d8a58ee02d72ecc7d4524d9cc306b9626b17ebe5b", "unit_id": "tag:06GB:prose-0004"}
